As shown in the first panel of Figure 3 , ConceptACP begins with feature localization. To remove a specific concept, we first identify its corresponding features and the cross-attention block in which they are most strongly represented. Prior work [ 33 ] reports that four SAEs trained on cross-attention blocks provide interpretable representations of distinct visual elements. Guided by this observation, we use publicly available general-purpose SAEs as the candidate pool to localize the features and target block associated with the concept, without requiring additional SAE training. Notably, ConceptACP can also utilize task-specific SAEs, although the publicly available SAEs already provide sufficiently informative features for precise localization in our experiments. Although it is possible to identify features related to a given concept by examining the feature heatmaps or manipulating feature activations to observe the resulting changes in generated images, this process is time-consuming and unreliable, particularly when concept-related activations are weak or ambiguous. Moreover, while SAEs promote sparse representations, a given concept may still correspond to multiple features, particularly when training data for that concept is limited or when the concept involves diverse visual elements. As shown in Figure 4 , our preliminary analysis indicates that feature #4913 on down.2.1 can effectively remove Nudity , whereas suppressing any one of the top-5 most activated features individually fails to fully eliminate Church . Fig. 4: Effect of suppressing key features on generated images. Suppressing #4913 removes nudity, while no single feature fully removes the Church concept. To address these challenges, inspired by the feature selection strategy in SAEUron [ 22 ] , we further develop an automated feature localization algorithm to locate relevant features and the target block. Specifically, we compute the statistical activations of features over subsets of relevant and irrelevant data, assigning each feature a score based on the differences in their activation distributions, where a higher score indicates a stronger correlation with the target concept. Using this scoring mechanism, we identify both the cross-attention block and the specific features representing the concept to be erased, forming the basis for the subsequent fine-tuning stage. Unlike SAEUron, which predefines the cross-attention block for each concept, we automatically search across a broader pool of SAEs and use concept-specific thresholds for more flexible selection. We denote the dataset containing the target concept as 𝒟 c \mathcal{D}_{c} and the dataset excluding it as 𝒟 ¬ c \mathcal{D}_{
