As discussed in § III-D , existing MU methods primarily rely on output-level metrics, such as the Forgetting Success Rate, which may not fully reflect changes in internal representations due to non-standardized evaluation datasets [ 34 ] and discrepancies between classifier predictions and human perception [ 44 ] . Consequently, output-level metrics provide limited insight into whether the target concept has been erased from internal representations, making it difficult to understand the internal mechanisms behind unlearning. To complement output-level evaluation, we consider an internal perspective that examines how model representations change during unlearning. Rather than introducing a standalone benchmark, our goal is to provide a post-hoc analysis tool that can be applied to different unlearning methods, helping interpret their effectiveness from the perspective of internal representation changes. To this end, we use SAEs as a unified analysis tool for probing intermediate representations. SAEs model the residual between input and output latents of a given block at a diffusion timestep, learning a sparse and interpretable feature basis. Prior work [ 33 ] shows that these learned features generalize across closely related models; for example, SAEs trained on SDXL can be directly applied to SDXL-Turbo at the same block, suggesting that they capture stable and semantically meaningful patterns rather than model-specific artifacts. Based on this property, we treat SAEs as a fixed feature space for analyzing model behavior before and after unlearning, independent of the specific MU method. Since the semantic meaning of SAE features remains largely consistent, changes in their activations can directly reflect how unlearning affects internal representations. From this perspective, we define two complementary internal metrics that quantify changes in feature activations induced by unlearning. These metrics serve as diagnostic tools rather than replacements for output-level evaluation, as they are measured in an SAE-derived feature space and may thus inherently favor SAE-based approaches. FO Score. We introduce the Feature Overlap (FO) Score , which measures how the most activated SAE features change before and after unlearning. Specifically, for the cross-attention module associated with the target concept, we use its corresponding SAE to extract the top- K K activated features under two settings: prompts containing the target concept c c and prompts excluding it. We denote the resulting feature sets before and after unlearning as ℱ c pre \mathcal{F}_{c}^{\mathrm{pre}} , ℱ c post \mathcal{F}_{c}^{\mathrm{post}} , ℱ ¬ c pre \mathcal{F}_{
